mungo-caverns -- grounding
======================

WHAT THIS IS
------------
mungo-caverns is a Shoddy port of a classic cave crawl (see LICENSE in
this directory for the full lineage and credits).  The C original lives
at C:\github\open-adventure-master and is the reference for everything: its
tests/ directory holds the recorded transcripts, and advent.h's struct
game_t documents, in comments, what every abbreviated field means.

The port began as YAML plus a code generator.  That is over.  adventure.yaml
and gen.shoddy now sit in obsolete/ and are not run.  The generated Shoddy
IS the codebase now and is edited by hand.  Do not regenerate anything.
Do not consult adventure.yaml as though it were live -- consult the C.

Layout:

    mungo-caverns-core.shoddy       280 lines -- LCG, tokenizer, word classification
    mungo-caverns.shoddy           5150 lines, 477 Defs -- everything else
    mungo-caverns-messages.shoddy   213 messages + 213 hand-written Msg* accessors
    mungo-caverns-locations.shoddy  185 rooms
    mungo-caverns-objects.shoddy     70 objects
    mungo-caverns-travel.shoddy     878 travel rows + the per-room key table
    mungo-caverns-vocab.shoddy       76 motions, 58 actions
    mungo-caverns-tables.shoddy     hints, score classes, turn thresholds, obituaries
    test.shoddy            LCG, table sizes, parser -- and nothing below them

THE GOAL
--------
Make this a showpiece for the Shoddy language.  Concretely that means the
code should read as an argument for the language's type system: sum types
where the C had magic integers, records where it had parallel arrays,
namespaces where it had a global struct.  A reader should be able to open
one file and find one idea in it.

Faithfulness is not negotiable while doing so.  This is a port, and the
recorded transcripts are the specification.

GROUND TRUTH
------------
There is currently no working regression net below the parser.  The
upstream .chk transcripts became a stale oracle when dwarves were renamed
to curmudgeons.  Recovering them is the first task: pipe the game's output
through a curmudgeon->dwarf filter and diff against the C's tests/.  That
restores an external oracle, which is worth far more than golden files
snapshotted from today's build.

Until that harness exists, no refactoring step below is safe to take.

THE RANDOM STREAM IS LOAD-BEARING
---------------------------------
Ninety-two of the hundred-odd recorded transcripts pin the LCG with an
in-band "seed" command.  A refactor that draws one extra number, or skips
one draw, diverges every seeded transcript from that point on.  The traps
are specific and already documented in the source:

  - a nocurmudgeons travel rule encodes as cond_goto with argument 100.
    It always passes and still costs a draw (see GotoOrPct / CondSpends).
  - the pit-fall roll in PitCheck is guarded by three tests before it;
    drawing unconditionally shifts the stream.
  - set_seed consumes exactly five randrange(26) draws building the magic
    word before returning (SeedState).

Any change near travel conditions or curmudgeon movement must be checked
against seeded transcripts, not just played by hand.

THE SAVE FORMAT IS FIXED
------------------------
WriteSaveAs enumerates every field with an explicit PutNum/PutBool.  It is
a positional write sequence, not reflection over the record.  Therefore
renaming fields, and nesting Game into sub-records, are both byte-safe --
only accessor paths move.  Reordering or adding PutNum calls is not.

DESIGN CHOICES ALREADY MADE
---------------------------

1.  Full names, no abbreviations.
    Every field, every Def.  The C's own comments in advent.h supply the
    meanings: dflag is the curmudgeon activation ladder, trnluz is points
    lost to turns, clshnt is "has read the endgame clue", abbnum is how
    often the long description prints, wzdark is whether the room being
    left was dark.  Recover the names, do not invent them.

    The qualifier comes from the namespace, not from stutter.  Prefer
    Location In Curmudgeon over CurmudgeonLocation.

2.  Namespaces on code modules, NOT on the data tables.
    Include "f.shoddy" As Q prefixes everything the file declares; a bare
    name resolves local -> own namespace -> exact -> unique bare candidate,
    and a cross-namespace clash is a compile error telling you to write
    Name In Q.

    But Tag is declared by Room, Obj, Vocab and Hint in four separate
    files.  Today they share one flat table and the accessor dispatches on
    the record's tag at runtime, which is why Tag(RoomAt(g)) and
    Tag(Nth(Actions(), i)) both work.  Namespacing those four files turns
    Tag into four distinct words and forces In at every call site.  Same
    trap for Words, Message, Turns, Text.

    So: data tables share one namespace, code modules get their own.

3.  Discriminated unions for every magic small integer.
    The rule of thumb: if a comment explains what the number means, the
    comment should have been a type.  Confirmed targets --

      Op.CondType / CondArg1 / CondArg2  ->  TravelCond
      Op.DestType / DestVal              ->  TravelDest
      DoSpecial's 1/2/3                  ->  Special
      Game.Bonus's 1/2/3                 ->  EndgameBonus
      Game.Dflag's 0..3+                 ->  CurmudgeonActivation
      Vocab.Tag string switch            ->  Verb

    Kind, Fate, Phase and Line already do this and are the pattern.

4.  Object props stay an Array of Number, with a view type over them.
    Tempting to give each object its own union; do not.  It is a rewrite,
    it changes the save format, and the stash trick (0 - 1 - prop) is
    behaviourally load-bearing.  Instead introduce

        Type PropState = Unfound(N) | Stashed(N) | InState(N)

    with PropView / SetPropView, so the < 0 and 0 - 1 - x arithmetic lives
    in two places instead of scattered across ~80 call sites.

5.  One result type for the turn loop.
    Type Done is Game x Phase.  Type Step is Game x Moved As Boolean.  They
    answer the same question and Resolved exists only to translate between
    them.  Collapse into one Phase-carrying result; Redescribes and
    WantsWord2 then delete themselves.  Found, Fleeing, Listened, Moved2
    and Tally3 are five more ad-hoc two-field results to fold in -- two of
    them are named with a digit because the good name was taken.

6.  Verb grammar belongs on the verb table row.
    NeedsObject, SpeaksTransitively and BigWord are three Contains({...})
    scans over string lists, held apart from the table they describe.
    Adding a verb today means four edits in four places.  Put the grammar
    on the row.

7.  Game gets named-field construction, then nesting.
    NewGame currently passes 40+ positional arguments across 15 lines.
    Inserting a field mid-record silently shifts every later argument --
    the single most dangerous construct in the codebase.  Named form first
    (Game(Location = 1, ...)), then group into Player / World / Clock /
    Score / Parse / Rng.

8.  Purity, last.
    mungo-caverns-core.shoddy promises "nothing here prints or reads", and one level
    up that promise is already broken: curmudgeons, pirate, hints, scoring
    and travel all call SpeakId from inside the rules.  That is why
    test.shoddy can only reach the parser and the LCG.  Making rules
    accumulate output instead of emitting it turns the game into
    Step(Game, Command) -> Game x Output and makes the transcript suite a
    pure-function test.  It is also the largest change here, touching most
    of 477 Defs.  It goes last, when the harness is trusted.

SHODDY HAZARDS TO KEEP IN MIND
------------------------------
  - Locals shadow accessors.  A parameter is looked up as written and is
    never namespaced, and the ambiguity check runs only after that miss.
    So a local named `location` silently captures the accessor Location,
    with no error.  Words are case-folded.  This is the one way a pure
    rename changes behaviour, and namespaces do not protect against it.
  - Builtins win over fields.  Avoid Length, Count, First, Last, Text,
    Value, Left, Right, Mid, Sort, Print as field names.  The codebase
    already skates on this: Hint.Number shadows a type name, and Step.Next,
    Op.Stop, Room.Long/Short are near-misses.
  - Record fields are positional.  See choice 7.
  - A wrapped And/Or/& line outside brackets grabs a stray stack value
    instead of erroring.
  - Rest copies a list, so structural recursion over one is quadratic.
    The existing code writes against Nth deliberately; keep doing that.
  - Nothing else in mills/, machines/ or tst/ uses As or In yet.  mungo-caverns
    will be the first consumer, so expect to find rough edges.

HOUSEKEEPING DEBT
-----------------
Every table file still opens with "Do not edit by hand: run build.cmd gen",
and build.cmd still has a gen target pointing into obsolete/.  Both are now
false and must go; the headers should be rewritten to document the data
instead.  Two defects found while surveying: Def ClosngPoints (misspelled,
one line below ClosingScore) and Def k2Of (the only lowercase-initial Def).

ORDER OF WORK
-------------
  1.  Transcript harness with the curmudgeon->dwarf filter.
  2.  Housekeeping: stale headers, gen target.
  3.  Split into modules and assign namespaces.  cave-messages loses its
      213 manual prefixes here (As Msg produces the identical names, so no
      call site changes).
  4.  The rename -- now per-namespace, short clean names, In where needed.
  5.  Cheap wins: load-time Lamp/Grate/Keys constants (ObjNamed linear-scans
      70 strings and Lamp is on the per-turn darkness path), seq.shoddy's
      IndexOf for IndexIn, merge duplicate ScanVocab/ScanObjects, List Of
      annotations throughout.
  6.  Travel-table unions.
  7.  Unify Step and Done.
  8.  Verb table.
  9.  Game named, then nested.
 10.  PropState view.
 11.  Output channel.

Steps 3 and 4 are deliberately in this order: namespacing is itself a
renaming mechanism, so renaming first with hand-written prefixes and then
stripping them during the split does the work twice.

RULES OF ENGAGEMENT
-------------------
  - Run the transcript harness after every step.  A step that cannot be
    verified is not finished.
  - Section comments carry the C file and line the rule came from.  Keep
    them accurate when code moves; they are what makes the two diffable
    by hand, and they are half the value of this port.
  - When the C and the port disagree, the C wins -- unless a recorded
    transcript says otherwise, in which case the transcript wins.
  - Do not "fix" behaviour that looks like a bug.  The original's bugs are
    load-bearing and the transcripts encode them.
  - Break work up into chunks to show progress
  - Use LOOP to execute the chunks; delay for only a few seconds
  - Stop and ask for clarifaction when needed.  
  - Dont change anything else without approval.

